**Process A (yellow, Ce⁴⁺‑rich)**  

1. Apply the steady‑state approximation (SSA) to the radical intermediate BrO₂•  

\[
\frac{d[\mathrm{BrO_2^\bullet}]}{dt}=2v_{1}-v_{2}=0\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;v_{2}=2v_{1}
\]

\[
v_{1}=k_{1}[\mathrm{HBrO_2}][\mathrm{BrO_3^-}][\mathrm{H^+}],\qquad   
v_{2}=k_{2}[\mathrm{BrO_2^\bullet}][\mathrm{Ce^{3+}}][\mathrm{H^+}]
\]

2. SSA for HBrO₂ (the only other step that consumes HBrO₂ is the second‑order destruction  
\(2\,\mathrm{HBrO_2}\xrightarrow{k_{3}}\)):

\[
\frac{d[\mathrm{HBrO_2}]}{dt}=-v_{1}+v_{2}-2v_{3}=0
\]

Using \(v_{2}=2v_{1}\) from step 1:

\[
-v_{1}+2v_{1}-2v_{3}=0\;\Longrightarrow\;v_{1}=2v_{3}
\]

\[
k_{1}[\mathrm{HBrO_2}][\mathrm{BrO_3^-}][\mathrm{H^+}]
   =2\,k_{3}[\mathrm{HBrO_2}]^{2}
\]

Cancelling \([\mathrm{HBrO_2}]\) (non‑zero) gives the stationary concentration:

\[
[\mathrm{HBrO_2}]_{\!A}
   =\frac{k_{1}[\mathrm{BrO_3^-}][\mathrm{H^+}]}{2k_{3}}
   =\frac{(1.0\times10^{4}\,\mathrm{M^{-2}s^{-1}})(0.06\,\mathrm{M})(0.8\,\mathrm{M})}
          {2(4.0\times10^{7}\,\mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}})}
   \approx 6.0\times10^{-6}\;\mathrm{M}
\]

---

**Process B (colourless, Br⁻‑rich)**  

During Process B, step 3 is negligible (Process A does not occur). The relevant steps for HBrO₂ are:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{4:}&\;\mathrm{HBrO_2}+ \mathrm{Br^-}+ \mathrm{H^+}\xrightarrow{k_{4}}2\,\mathrm{HBrO}\\
\text{5:}&\;\mathrm{BrO_3^-}+ \mathrm{Br^-}+2\mathrm{H^+}\xrightarrow{k_{5}}\mathrm{HBrO}+ \mathrm{HBrO_2}
\end{aligned}
\]

SSA for HBrO₂ (formation = consumption) gives:

\[
v_{5}=v_{4}
\]

\[
k_{5}[\mathrm{BrO_3^-}][\mathrm{Br^-}][\mathrm{H^+}]^{2}
   =k_{4}[\mathrm{HBrO_2}][\mathrm{Br^-}][\mathrm{H^+}]
\]

Cancelling \([\mathrm{Br^-}]\) and one \([\mathrm{H^+}]\) yields:

\[
[\mathrm{HBrO_2}]_{\!B}
   =\frac{k_{5}[\mathrm{BrO_3^-}][\mathrm{H^+}]}{k_{4}}
   =\frac{(2.1\,\mathrm{M^{-3}s^{-1}})(0.06\,\mathrm{M})(0.8\,\mathrm{M})}
          {2.0\times10^{9}\,\mathrm{M^{-2}s^{-1}}}
   \approx 5.0\times10^{-11}\;\mathrm{M}
\]

---

**Result**

\[
\boxed{[\mathrm{HBrO_2}]_{\!A}\approx 6\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}},\qquad
\boxed{[\mathrm{HBrO_2}]_{\!B}\approx 5\times10^{-11}\ \text{M}}
\]

These are the stationary HBrO₂ concentrations in Processes A and B under the stated assumptions.  

FINAL ANSWER: [HBrO₂]_A ≈ 6 × 10⁻⁶ M; [HBrO₂]_B ≈ 5 × 10⁻¹¹ M.

**F** – A cyclohexa‑2,5‑dien‑1‑ol bearing a 4‑OTBS‑biphenyl group at C‑1 and a p‑bromophenyl substituent together with a free –OH at C‑4 (i.e. 1‑(4‑OTBS‑biphenyl)‑4‑(p‑bromophenyl)‑4‑hydroxy‑cyclohexa‑2,5‑dien‑1‑ol).

**G** – Both free –OH groups of **F** are protected as triethylsilyl (TES) ethers; the OTBS group on the biphenyl remains (1‑(TES‑O)‑4‑(TES‑O)‑1‑(4‑OTBS‑biphenyl)‑4‑(p‑bromophenyl)‑cyclohexa‑2,5‑diene).

**H** – Selective hydrolysis of one of the TES groups gives a mono‑TES‑protected diol; the OTBS on the biphenyl is still present (1‑(TES‑O)‑4‑OH‑1‑(4‑OTBS‑biphenyl)‑4‑(p‑bromophenyl)‑cyclohexa‑2,5‑diene).

**I** – Oxidation of the diol with PhI(OAc)₂/H₂O converts the 1,4‑diol into a para‑quinone; the silyl groups are lost, giving 1‑(4‑OTBS‑biphenyl)‑4‑(p‑bromophenyl)‑cyclohexa‑2,5‑diene‑1,4‑dione (the OTBS‑quinone).

**J** – NaH deprotonates the quinone; addition of p‑lithio‑p‑bromobenzene to the carbonyl at C‑1 followed by work‑up yields a tertiary alcohol at C‑1 bearing a new p‑bromophenyl group. The other carbonyl (C‑4) remains, producing a hydroxy‑quinone with three aryl substituents (biphenyl‑OTBS, original p‑bromophenyl, and the newly introduced p‑bromophenyl).

**K** – Both free –OH groups of **J** (the newly formed tertiary alcohol and the phenolic OH at C‑4) are silylated with TESCl (2 equiv.), giving the di‑TES‑protected hydroxy‑quinone (1‑(TES‑O)‑4‑(TES‑O)‑1‑(4‑OTBS‑biphenyl)‑4‑(p‑bromophenyl)‑cyclohexa‑2,5‑diene‑1‑one‑4‑yl‑p‑bromophenyl).

**L** – Ni(COD)₂/2,2′‑bipyridine mediates intramolecular aryl‑aryl coupling of the three bromophenyl units in **K**, forming a five‑membered para‑phenylene macrocycle (tetra‑TES‑protected cyclopentaparaphenylene, C₅₄H₈₀O₄Si₄).

**FINAL ANSWER:**  
F = 1‑(4‑OTBS‑biphenyl)‑4‑(p‑bromophenyl)‑4‑hydroxy‑cyclohexa‑2,5‑dien‑1‑ol;  
G = its bis‑TES ether (both OH protected);  
H = mono‑TES‑protected diol (one TES, one free OH, OTBS present);  
I = para‑quinone (1‑(4‑OTBS‑biphenyl)‑4‑(p‑bromophenyl)‑cyclohexa‑2,5‑diene‑1,4‑dione);  
J = hydroxy‑quinone with three aryl substituents after addition of p‑lithio‑p‑bromobenzene;  
K = di‑TES‑protected hydroxy‑quinone;  
L = tetra‑TES‑protected cyclopentaparaphenylene (C₅₄H₈₀O₄Si₄).
